{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Doctor: mapping-config diagnostics with offline-always / live-drift-degrades portability\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThe mapping seam (S1–S8) introduces a hand-editable `[mapping]` config surface that can be\nmisconfigured in ways operators need surfaced before a reconcile drifts silently. Two distinct\nfailure classes exist:\n\n- **Internally-invalid config** — S1/S3/S4 already fail closed on these by raising\n  `MappingConfigError` (a `ConfigError` subclass): a non-integer `hierarchy` rank and a\n  malformed block fail at LOAD (`mapping_config.load_mapping_config`); an axis map value\n  outside its effective vocabulary, a capability-absent axis reference, and an\n  unmapped-non-skipped local type fail in the `rebar_reconciler.config` resolvers\n  (`effective_status_map` / `effective_type_map` / `assert_type_decisions_complete` /\n  `effective_link_map`, which inject the built-in `MappingLayer` + `Capability` and call\n  `mapping_config.validate`). Doctor should surface these as **error** findings rather than\n  letting them surface only at reconcile time.\n- **Live drift** — a config that is internally valid but *disagrees with the live Jira\n  project*: a mapped status/type/link target Jira no longer exposes. Doctor should surface\n  these as findings when Jira is reachable, and degrade cleanly when it is not.\n\n`rebar doctor` is the existing diagnostic home, so this child extends it rather than adding a\nnew command.\n\nThe extension must stay portable: `rebar doctor` runs where the optional **`jira-datacenter`**\nextra is not installed and where no Jira credentials are configured. It must never crash or\nhard-depend on Jira there — the offline config checks always run, and the live-drift check\ndegrades to an `unavailable` finding, mirroring how `rebar metrics` reports `unavailable` when a\nsource is absent (`docs/user-guide.md`).\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a mapping-diagnostics section to `src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py`, wired into `scan`/the\ndoctor CLI, split into two tiers by dependency. Mapping findings are collected into their **own\nlist** (as doctor already does for its scan-dirty and lock reports), rendered and folded into\nthe exit code alongside the existing findings, and kept **out of** `run_repair`'s iteration — so\n`doctor.scan()`'s existing blocking-edge finding contract (and `run_repair` / `repair_finding`\n/ `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor.py`) is unchanged.\n\n- **Offline checks (always run, stdlib-only, no Jira import):** run the provider-neutral\n  `rebar_reconciler.config` resolvers — `effective_status_map`, `effective_type_map`,\n  `assert_type_decisions_complete`, `effective_link_map` — against the loaded config. These\n  supply the built-in `MappingLayer` (`local_to_jira_status` / `LOCAL_TYPE_TO_JIRA` / the link\n  literal) and the `Capability`, import `mapping_config` lazily, and are stdlib-only at import\n  (they never touch `adapters.jira*`). Any `MappingConfigError` they raise — non-integer\n  hierarchy, malformed block, out-of-vocabulary status/type/link value, capability-absent axis\n  reference, or an unmapped-non-skipped type — becomes an **error** finding naming the offending\n  key (non-zero exit). In addition, report one genuinely-*soft* problem that is NOT a load\n  error: a `projects.<KEY>` block whose axis maps and vocabulary are all empty (a likely stub),\n  detected by inspecting the loaded `MappingConfig` structurally — surfaced as a **warning**.\n- **Live-drift check (best-effort, degrades):** run only when the `jira-datacenter` extra is\n  importable — gated through the established seam\n  `rebar._optional.capability_installed(\"jira_datacenter\")` — AND credentials resolve. On the\n  available path, reuse S8's read-only probe reader (`mapping_probe.build_probe`, backed by\n  `resolve_jira_datacenter_settings`) and diff the configured vocabulary against observed Jira\n  vocabulary, emitting one finding per divergence on the axes the reader actually observes:\n  **status, type, and link** (a configured value absent from Jira's observed set). Hierarchy is\n  deliberately **excluded** from the live diff: `build_probe` resolves Data Center settings, and\n  `mapping_probe._hierarchy` documents `hierarchyLevel` as Cloud-only — on Data Center it is\n  always empty and `hierarchy` is dropped, so a hierarchy divergence is structurally\n  undetectable against this reader (offline hierarchy validity is still covered by the offline\n  tier). When the extra is absent, OR the resolved PAT is empty (`JIRA_PAT` unset —\n  `resolve_jira_datacenter_settings` is total and returns `pat=\"\"`), OR settings resolution\n  raises `ConfigError` (it fails loud on a malformed `[tool.rebar.reconciler]` section), OR the\n  probe transport raises, emit a single `unavailable` finding (\"live mapping-drift check\n  skipped: <reason>\") and return success — never raise. The Jira import and settings/probe\n  construction are performed lazily inside the check, behind the capability guard and wrapped so\n  every one of those degradation causes folds into the one `unavailable` finding.\n\nFindings integrate with doctor's severity/exit-code convention: offline\n`MappingConfigError`-class problems are errors; the soft stub-block problem is a warning; the\n`unavailable` live check is informational and does not fail the command.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py` — new mapping-diagnostics function collecting into its own\n  findings list; the `capability_installed(\"jira_datacenter\")` guard and lazy Jira import.\n- `docs/user-guide.md` — document the new mapping diagnostics: the offline error/warning\n  findings and the `unavailable` live-drift convention.\n- Reuses the S1 `mapping_config` module via the `rebar_reconciler.config` resolvers, and S8's\n  `mapping_probe.build_probe` reader; adds no new config surface.\n- Out of scope: `--repair` auto-fixing mapping config (report-only this child); adding Jira as a\n  hard dependency of doctor; the live-drift **true path against real Jira** — E2E transport is\n  S8's proven read-only probe reader (its contract is covered by S8's own tests), so this child\n  proves the diff logic through an injected vocabulary and treats the real-transport path as\n  explicitly not-applicable here.\n\n## Testing\n\n- **Unit (offline tier), no Jira import:** feed `doctor` a temp config with (a) a valid mapping\n  → no mapping findings; (b) each error-class problem (non-integer hierarchy, out-of-vocabulary\n  map value, unmapped-non-skipped type) → asserts an **error** finding naming the offending key\n  and a non-zero exit; (c) the soft stub problem (an all-empty `projects.<KEY>` block) → asserts\n  a **warning** finding naming the key. Oracle: the finding list and exit code. Asserts no\n  `adapters.jira` import occurs on this path.\n- **Unit (portability tier):** force the availability guard false (monkeypatch\n  `capability_installed` for `\"jira_datacenter\"`) → asserts exactly one `unavailable` finding, a\n  success exit, and that no Jira import is attempted. Oracle: the single `unavailable` finding +\n  zero exit.\n- **Unit (degradation causes):** with the guard true, drive each non-transport degradation\n  cause — empty resolved PAT, and a `ConfigError` from settings resolution — and assert each\n  folds into exactly one `unavailable` finding with the matching reason and a success exit.\n  Oracle: the single `unavailable` finding + reason + zero exit.\n- **Unit (live-drift tier), injected transport:** inject a fake probe reader returning a\n  vocabulary that diverges from the configured one on the status, type, and link axes → asserts\n  one drift finding per divergence, each naming the axis and the absent value. No live Jira.\n  Oracle: the per-axis finding set.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Running `rebar doctor` on a config with a valid `[mapping]` section and the\n  `jira-datacenter` extra absent completes successfully and emits exactly one `unavailable`\n  finding for the live-drift check — verified by the portability-tier unit test (guard forced\n  false via `capability_installed`, asserting no Jira import and zero exit).\n- [ ] Each offline error-class problem (non-integer hierarchy, out-of-vocabulary map value,\n  unmapped-non-skipped type) produces an **error** finding that names the offending key and a\n  non-zero exit — verified by the offline-tier unit test running the\n  `rebar_reconciler.config` resolvers and asserting the `MappingConfigError`-derived finding.\n- [ ] An all-empty `projects.<KEY>` stub block produces a **warning** finding naming the key\n  (not an error, not a non-zero exit) — verified by the offline-tier unit test.\n- [ ] Each non-transport live-check degradation cause — `jira-datacenter` extra absent, empty\n  resolved PAT (`JIRA_PAT` unset), and a `ConfigError` from settings resolution — folds into a\n  single `unavailable` finding with a reason and a success exit — verified by the portability\n  and degradation-cause unit tests.\n- [ ] With a fake probe reader injected, each configured-vs-observed divergence on the status,\n  type, and link axes produces a drift finding naming the axis and absent value — verified by\n  the live-drift-tier unit test. (Hierarchy is excluded from the live diff by design: the\n  reused Data Center probe reader never observes `hierarchyLevel`.)\n- [ ] The doctor module imports and `rebar doctor` runs to completion with the\n  `jira-datacenter` extra uninstalled — verified by the portability-tier test asserting the Jira\n  import is lazy and guarded (no import on the offline/unavailable path).\n- [ ] `docs/user-guide.md` documents the new mapping diagnostics (offline error/warning\n  findings and the `unavailable` live-drift convention).\n\nTest tiers: **unit (offline, jira-absent)**, **unit (portability, guard-forced-false)**, **unit\n(degradation causes)**, and **unit (live-drift, injected transport)** — every behavior is\nexercised in-process with no live Jira and no specific CI provider, satisfying\n`project.portability`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787389462295887001,"uuid":"21ddc9d3-3bd5-49e4-b5b9-08fa59b403ab"}